  "inShort": {
    "summary": "Comments want prosthetics shown as something people wear and manage, not only as something that moves on screen. Posts get praise when they explain care, comfort and movement clearly.",
    "figures": [
      {
        "value": "521",
        "label": "videos in your market in September"
      },
      {
        "value": "3.2%",
        "label": "median engagement on the category's story videos, against 2.6% for all its videos"
      }
    ],
    "also": [
      {
        "headline": "Buyers want prosthetic type choices explained for their own body and life",
        "brief": "sales"
      },
      {
        "headline": "Provider search, approval and coverage shape whether buyers can get a prosthesis",
        "brief": "sales"
      },
      {
        "headline": "People do not treat prosthetic mechanics as obvious, they ask what each part does",
        "brief": "marketing"
      },
      {
        "headline": "Pain, fit and skin problems decide whether owners keep wearing a prosthesis",
        "brief": "leadership"
      },
      {
        "headline": "Ottobock is known for microprocessor knees tied to smooth movement and independence",
        "brief": "leadership"
      }
    ]
  },
  "findings": [
    {
      "ideaId": "I2",
      "headline": "Comments ask to see daily wear, from putting it on to bathroom and water use",
      "basedOn": [
        "G57",
        "G80",
        "G87",
        "G93",
        "G100"
      ],
      "saw": [
        "People ask for the wearing routine in plain steps. Comments want to see how a prosthesis is put on, what socks or suction do, what accessories are for, how a locking system attaches, how it stays secure, and whether it comes off for sleep or other care.",
        "People ask about living in it through the day. Comments ask about joint movement, showering, bathroom use, water, clothing, stump and liner care, skin irritation, pain, weight, comfort across a full day, phantom sensation, and what wearing one feels like. People also ask how children are fitted, whether children outgrow devices, how wear changes across body types or bilateral use, and why some people choose not to wear a prosthesis."
      ],
      "means": "People are not treating wear as obvious once the device is fitted. Basic use, care and removal are part of what they want shown, and posts that stay on finished movement leave ordinary ownership unanswered.",
      "practice": [
        "Comments treat bathroom, bathing and sleep as ordinary use.",
        "Comments ask about socks, liners, covers and stockings as part of the wearing routine."
      ],
